This is probably a pretty niche use case, so I'm floating this as a discussion than feature.

I'm working on [`roxylint`](https://github.com/openpharma/roxylint), which provides some linters for `roxygen2`. So far, these have operated on the raw text of the header, but that approach is only useful for simple docs. With more involved docs that have extensive markdown or inline Rd tags, this approach can raise lots of false positive lints.

I'm trying to improve this by walking the tree of the derived Rd, but this makes it much harder to back out source code locations where the lints occurred.

Would it be possible to introduce an RdMacro that renders transparently, but encodes the source code location of the text? In most cases, I imagine this is unnecessary when generating package docs, but if it could be optionally enabled to wrap all tags in this RdMacro, that would allow me to provide far more helpful lints. 

Something like

```r
tag <- roxy_tag_parse(roxy_tag("param", "`character` A value."))
roxy_tag_rd(tag, keep.source = TRUE)
#> \arguments{
#> \item{
#>   {\roxygenSrcref{R/file.R}{1}{1}{1}{12}{\code{character}}}
#>   {\roxygenSrcref{R/file.R}{1}{1}{1}{12}{A value.}}
#> }
```

With

```tex
\newcommand{roxygenSrcref}{#6}
```

It's still quite hypothetical, so I'm just floating the idea to gauge interest.

